Predictions are cached, so changing the selected store is fast after the first load.

## 7. Feature dictionary

All 23 model inputs, grouped by type.

**Store and promotion information (from the data)**

| Feature | Notes |
|---|---|
| `Store` | Store ID |
| `Promo` | Promotion running on the target day |
| `StateHoliday`, `SchoolHoliday` | Holiday flags (`StateHoliday` is label-encoded) |
| `StoreType`, `Assortment`, `PromoInterval` | Label-encoded categories |
| `CompetitionDistance` | Distance to nearest competitor |
| `CompetitionOpenSinceMonth`, `CompetitionOpenSinceYear` | 0 if unknown |
| `Promo2`, `Promo2SinceWeek`, `Promo2SinceYear` | Promo2 programme details |

**Calendar features**

| Feature | Notes |
|---|---|
| `DayOfWeek` | 1 to 7 |
| `Year`, `Month`, `Day` | From the date |
| `WeekOfYear` | ISO week number |

**History features (built from past sales of the same store)**

| Feature | Definition |
|---|---|
| `lag_7` | Sales 7 days earlier |
| `lag_14` | Sales 14 days earlier |
| `lag_28` | Sales 28 days earlier |
| `roll_mean_7` | Average of the 7 days ending 7 days before the target day |
| `roll_mean_28` | Average of the 28 days ending 7 days before the target day |

The rolling means are shifted by 7 days, so every history feature is at least 7 days old. This is what makes the task a genuine 7-day-ahead forecast and prevents leakage.

**Encoding:** text columns are converted with pandas category codes. The codes are consistent within a run, because the same data is always encoded together.

## 8. Modelling and evaluation

### Validation strategy

The data is split **by time**, never randomly:
- **Train:** all rows before 2015-06-19
- **Validation:** 2015-06-19 to 2015-07-31 (about 6 weeks)

A random split would let the model see future days during training and would give unrealistically good scores.

**Cross-validation:** the notebook also runs 3 expanding-window folds. Each fold trains on everything before its start date and validates on the next period:

| Fold | Validation period |
|---|---|
| 1 | 2015-03-20 to 2015-05-01 |
| 2 | 2015-05-01 to 2015-06-12 |
| 3 | 2015-06-12 to 2015-07-31 |

### Metrics

| Metric | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **MAE** | Mean absolute error: the average miss in sales units. Easy to interpret |
| **RMSE** | Root mean squared error: like MAE but punishes large misses more |
| **MAPE %** | Mean absolute percentage error: the average miss as a percentage of actual sales (rows with 0 actual sales are skipped) |

### Models

| Model | Description |
|---|---|
| Last week (`lag_7`) | Predict the same sales as 7 days ago |
| Two weeks ago (`lag_14`) | Predict the same sales as 14 days ago |
| Moving average (28d) | Predict the 28-day rolling mean |
| Linear Regression | Linear model on the same features |
| LightGBM | Gradient-boosted decision trees on the raw sales target |
| LightGBM (log target) | Trained on `log(1 + Sales)`, then predictions are converted back with `expm1`. Reduces the effect of very large sales values |
| LightGBM (tuned, log target) | Log-target model with the best settings from tuning (the final model) |

### Hyperparameter tuning

Three configurations were compared on the validation window:

| n_estimators | learning_rate | num_leaves | Validation MAE |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1000 | 0.03 | 63 | 621.1 |
| **800** | **0.05** | **127** | **603.0** |
| 800 | 0.05 | 31 | 637.0 |

Fixed settings for all LightGBM models: `subsample=0.8`, `subsample_freq=1`, `colsample_bytree=0.8`, `random_state=42`.

## 9. Results

Validation window: 2015-06-19 to 2015-07-31.

| Model | MAE | RMSE | MAPE % |
|---|---|---|---|
| **LightGBM (tuned, log target)** | **603.0** | **894.8** | **9.03** |
| LightGBM (log target) | 610.7 | 890.5 | 9.19 |
| LightGBM | 620.1 | 900.4 | 9.45 |
| Linear Regression | 824.1 | 1139.3 | 12.82 |
| Two weeks ago (`lag_14`) | 813.0 | 1183.2 | 11.77 |
| Moving average (28 days) | 1489.4 | 2106.2 | 20.44 |
| Last week (`lag_7`) | 2174.7 | 2799.2 | 31.80 |

**Cross-validation MAE (tuned model):** 899.0, 743.5 and 614.9 across the three folds, a **mean of 752.4**.

### How to read the results

- The final model has about 27% lower MAE than Linear Regression and far lower error than the naive baselines.
- The MAE of 603 is measured on a window that was also used to choose the hyperparameters. The cross-validation mean of 752.4 is the more reliable estimate of future performance.
- Early folds have higher error because the model has less training data there.
- **Why `lag_7` is a poor baseline:** a store's promo flag matches the one from 14 days ago 85% of the time, but the one from 7 days ago only 35% of the time. Stores alternate promotion weeks, so the same weekday two weeks ago is a better guide. This is also why `lag_14` is the most important feature (by gain), followed by `lag_28`, `roll_mean_28` and `Promo`.

### Exploratory findings

- Sales drop sharply each Sunday because most stores close.
- Average sales peak in December, with a smaller rise in spring and a dip from August to October.
- Average sales are about 38% higher on promo days.

